window.__vaultPreview("d34d35560323bb1b",{"text":"# Supporting Blog Brief B1: Salt-Based vs Salt-Free Water Softeners in Arizona\n\nPrepared 2026-09-18. Brief only. Nothing written or published yet.\n\nHub post this supports: `/blog/are-water-softeners-worth-it/` (233 impressions, position 13.7 over 3 months; 59 impressions at position 8.5 in the last 28 days).\nMoney pages: `/water-softeners/`, then the reader's city page (`/water-softener-installation-mesa/` and siblings).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Keyword research (Search Atlas, US, Sep 18 2026)\n\n| Keyword | Volume | KD | CPC | Read |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| salt based vs salt free water softener | 720 | 23 | $3.75 | The parent term. Low difficulty for its size. |\n| water conditioner vs water softener | 1,000 | 35 | $4.67 | Same question in other words. Use as a secondary heading. |\n| what size water softener do i need | 720 | 16 | $3.56 | Belongs to B2, link across. |\n| do salt free water softeners work | 210 | 33 | $2.81 | Direct question, good as an H2. |\n| water softener phoenix az | 880 | 16 | $22.86 | Money term. Belongs to the service page, not the blog. |\n| water softener mesa az | 50 | 13 | $21.91 | Money term. Service page. |\n| salt free water softener arizona | 10 | 6 | n/a | Low reported volume, but see the SERP check below. |\n| salt free water softener phoenix | 10 | 7 | n/a | Same. |\n| water softener vs water conditioner arizona | 0 | 18 | n/a | Long-tail framing only. |\n| hard water arizona | 10 | 12 | n/a | Supporting phrase. |\n| best water softener for arizona hard water | 0 | 7 | n/a | Answer inside the post, do not build a heading on it. |\n\n**Google SERP check (Sep 18, US, Mesa location hint)**\n- **\"salt based vs salt free water softener\"**: AI Overview present, People Also Ask present. The top 7 are equipment manufacturers and dealers plus Reddit. A plumbing company is unlikely to take this nationally.\n- **\"do salt free water softeners work arizona\"**: AI Overview present, PAA present. The top 8 are **Arizona** water-treatment companies and Arizona plumbers, including two names that already appear in Summit's own Semrush keyword list. This is the winnable version.\n\n**Decision:** write the post Arizona-first. The tools report low volume on the Arizona-modified phrases because volume sits on the national parent, but the Arizona SERP is local and beatable, and Arizona searchers see local results for the parent term too.\n\n**Primary keyword:** salt free water softener arizona\n**Secondary:** salt based vs salt free water softener, water conditioner vs water softener, do salt free water softeners work\n**Do not target here:** water softener phoenix az, water softener mesa az (money terms owned by the service pages).\n\n**GSC support from the hub** (3 months): \"water softener cost vs benefit phoenix\" 10 impressions at 22.6, \"benefits of water softener phoenix\" 6 at 47, \"do i need a water softener in arizona\" 2 at 43 with the hub's only click, \"best water softener system for arizona\" 1 at 4. The hub is being asked comparison questions it only half answers.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Intent, reader and job\n\n- **Intent:** informational, comparison, pre-purchase. Not transactional.\n- **Reader:** an East Valley homeowner who accepts they have hard water and is choosing between a salt-based softener and a salt-free conditioner. Often has a pre-plumbed softener loop in the garage.\n- **Job of the page:** answer the comparison honestly, then hand the reader to the service page when they want it installed.\n- **Why it fits the hub:** the hub answers \"is it worth it\", this answers \"which type\". Different question, same reader, one step later.\n\n---\n\n## 3. AEO: the questions the page must answer outright\n\nTaken from the live PAA boxes on the two SERPs above. Each becomes an H2 or an FAQ, and each gets a direct answer in the first 2 to 3 sentences (about 40 to 60 words) before any detail.\n\n1. Do salt-free water softening systems really work?\n2. What is the difference between a water softener and a water conditioner?\n3. Are saltless water softeners worth it in Arizona?\n4. What are the disadvantages of salt-based water softeners?\n5. Which is better for Arizona hard water?\n6. How long do saltless systems last?\n7. Do salt-free systems protect a tankless water heater?\n8. What does a salt-free system cost? (answer without prices: explain what drives the cost, then link to `/contact/` for a free estimate)\n\n---\n\n## 4. GEO: getting quoted by AI answers\n\n- AI Overviews appear on all three queries checked, so the page must be extractable: one clear claim per paragraph, short sentences, no build-up before the answer.\n- Use precise, citable local numbers rather than \"Arizona water is hard\": City of Mesa hardness about 12 to 16 grains per gallon by zone (2025 water quality report), EPCOR San Tan about 15 to 18 gpg. Both were verified on Sep 14 and must be re-checked on the writing day.\n- Name the entities plainly in the copy: Summit Plumbing Pros, Mesa, East Valley, City of Mesa, EPCOR, AZ ROC 338366. AI systems match brands to places through this kind of plain statement.\n- Add the FAQ block using the existing accordion pattern so the FAQ schema matches the visible text.\n- Link out once to a neutral authority (the city or utility water quality report). No competitor links.\n- **After publishing:** add 3 prompts to the LLM Visibility project (`summitplumbingpros.com`, 36 tracked queries today, none about softeners):\n  - \"Do salt-free water softeners work in Arizona hard water?\"\n  - \"Salt vs salt-free water softener for a Mesa AZ home\"\n  - \"Best water softener setup for East Valley hard water\"\n\n---\n\n## 5. Proposed structure\n\n| Section | Purpose | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| H1: Salt-Based vs Salt-Free Water Softeners in Arizona Homes | Primary keyword, Arizona framing | |\n| Intro (60 to 80 words) | Answer up front: salt-based softens, salt-free conditions. Say plainly which problem each one solves. | No throat-clearing |\n| H2: What \"salt-free\" actually means | Define conditioning vs ion exchange | Quotable definition sentence |\n| H2: Do salt-free systems work in Arizona hard water? | Direct answer, then the conditions where each performs | Cites Mesa and EPCOR hardness numbers |\n| H2: What changes in your home with each one | Scale, spotting, soap use, appliance wear, skin feel | Plain observations, no guarantees |\n| H2: Maintenance each one needs | Salt refills and regeneration vs media replacement | Links to B3 when it exists |\n| H2: Drains, septic and local rules | Brine discharge, septic considerations | Verify any rule before stating it |\n| H2: Which one suits your home | Short decision list (water hardness, tankless heater, septic, salt handling, space) | The section AI answers tend to quote |\n| H2: Frequently asked questions | The 8 questions in section 3 | Accordion, schema follows the visible text |\n| Closing CTA | One line plus a link to a free estimate | No urgency language, no emergency claims |\n\n- Length: 900 to 1,200 words. Long enough to cover the comparison, short enough to stay specific.\n- Featured image: use an existing library image or a new branded banner. Alt text describing the image, including \"water softener\" naturally.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Metadata (draft, for approval)\n\n- **Slug:** `/blog/salt-free-vs-salt-based-water-softener-arizona/`\n- **SEO title:** Salt-Free vs Salt-Based Water Softeners in Arizona (57 characters)\n- **Meta description:** Salt-free conditioners and salt-based softeners handle Arizona hard water differently. See what each one changes, what it costs you in upkeep, and which fits. (158 characters)\n- **Rank Math focus keyword:** salt free water softener arizona\n- **Category:** Plumbing Tips (or a water-quality category if you prefer; the blog currently has 4 categories)\n\n---\n\n## 7. Internal links\n\n| # | Source | Destination | Anchor | Placement |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| B1-a | New post | /blog/are-water-softeners-worth-it/ | are water softeners worth it in Arizona homes | Intro, after the first answer |\n| B1-b | New post | /water-softeners/ | water softener installation | Closing section, above the CTA |\n| B1-c | /blog/are-water-softeners-worth-it/ | New post | salt-based and salt-free systems | In the section that introduces system types |\n| B1-d | /blog/signs-of-hard-water-in-your-home/ | New post | which type of softener suits Arizona water | After the symptoms list |\n| B1-e | New post | /blog/tankless-water-heater-descaling-arizona/ | descaling a tankless water heater | In the tankless question, only if it reads naturally |\n\nNo city-to-city links, no exact-match anchors repeated, no links added only for SEO.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Rules this draft will follow\n\n- Arizona homeowners, short paragraphs, plain words, no em dashes.\n- No prices, no discounts, no warranties, no years of experience, no certifications beyond AZ ROC 338366.\n- No emergency, 24/7, after-hours or guaranteed same-day language.\n- No brand names of softener manufacturers, no competitor mentions, no invented reviews or project examples.\n- Every local figure cited to the city or utility report, verified on the writing day.\n- No claims about health benefits of soft or conditioned water.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Publishing checklist (after your approval of the draft)\n\n1. Draft in full for your review, including the FAQ answers.\n2. On approval: publish as a post, category set, featured image with alt text.\n3. Rank Math: title, description, focus keyword as above.\n4. Add links B1-c and B1-d to the two existing posts (separate approval, they change live pages).\n5. Clear the Rank Math sitemap cache, confirm the URL appears in `post-sitemap.xml`.\n6. GSC: Request Indexing.\n7. Add the 3 GEO prompts to the LLM Visibility project.\n8. Log everything in `Summit-AZ-SEO-Change-Log.csv`.\n9. Re-check at the Oct 2 review: hub position, new post impressions, `/water-softeners/` position.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Approval\n\nSay \"approve B1 draft\" and I will write the full post, in Summit's voice, with the FAQ answers and the exact link sentences, for your review before anything goes live.\n"});
